================================================================================
AdaRound: adding an OptimDevice leaves the cpu path bit-for-bit unchanged
================================================================================
Date        : 2026-09-09 13:22
Machine     : DESKTOP-CBL5NUA (Desktop 2), Ryzen 7 8700G, Phoenix XDNA1
Env         : resnet_env (torch 2.4.1+cpu)
Branch      : worktree-ignition-dev
Hardware    : NONE on the NPU. The only onnxruntime use is Quark's own CPU
              activation extraction; no VitisAI session, no hardware context.
Host load   : OMP_NUM_THREADS=4. This is a fixture run (a 2-channel 4x4 Conv,
              one layer), not a producer pass.

WHAT THIS IS NOT
----------------
There is NO GPU measurement here and none was possible on this machine. resnet_env
carries torch 2.4.1+cpu -- a cpu-only build, no CUDA and no HIP -- and Desktop 2's
GPU is a Radeon 780M iGPU. The RX 7900 XTX the GPU path targets is on DESKTOP 1.
Nothing in this log says AdaRound is faster on a GPU, or that it runs on one at all.

What it does establish is the precondition: that adding the device plumbing did not
disturb the cpu path, which is the one that is byte-identical to a fresh Quark
XINT8_ADAROUND oracle. That property is the reason quant/adaround.py exists, and a
silent regression in it is the only serious risk the refactor carried.

METHOD
------
tools/quant_adaround_device_checks.py runs the transcription on a synthetic QDQ Conv
built by tools/quant_fixtures.py::adaround_pair, whose float weights sit deliberately
off the quantization grid so AdaRound has a real rounding decision to make. The same
script is pointed at two checkouts with --repo: the pre-change code in the main
checkout at 311a672, and the post-change code in this worktree. It compares the
emitted int8 weights, the per-layer report figures, and every transcribed log line.

Ignition's own ADAROUND banner is excluded from the trace comparison and named as
such in the tool: it is this project's telemetry, not transcribed Quark output, and
it gained optim_device/byte_parity_path fields on purpose. Everything Quark itself
prints -- "Module ...", "adaround iterations=...", "LAYER ..." -- is compared exactly.
