--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
READING
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
The emitted int8 weights, the recon metrics, the changed-element counts and every
transcribed log line are identical between the two checkouts at both settings. The
200-iteration case moves 16 of 36 weights, so the loop is genuinely exercised rather
than terminating early on an untouched tensor.

The guard that matters most is the last one: an OptimDevice torch cannot reach raises
RuntimeError naming the torch build, instead of silently falling back to the cpu. A
"GPU run" that is quietly a cpu run is the same failure shape as this repo's CPU run
in an NPU costume (npu.session.resolve_xclbin), and it is refused the same way.

STILL OPEN
----------
- No GPU has run this. Whether a Radeon RX 7900 XTX shortens the 1000-iteration loop
  on a wide model is unmeasured, and so is whether a torch build that can reach it
  exists on Windows. torch's official ROCm wheels are Linux-only; on Windows the
  candidates are AMD's ROCm-on-Windows preview or torch-directml, and neither is
  installed on any machine here. Installing one is a Desktop 1 decision.
- A GPU run will NOT be byte-identical to the oracle, by construction. The config
  refuses to leave the cpu without AllowNonParityDevice, the report records
  byte_parity_path=false, and the run logs a warning. Compare accuracy, never bytes.
